    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Basaltic volcanic eruptions can commonly be characterized by three stages. Magma first rises through the upper kilometer of the crust in planar dikes, which result in fissure eruptions when they intersect the surface. In some cases, the fissures may not feed significant eruptions and may quickly die out. But in other fissures, magma may continue to erupt and become localized into discrete vents, until finally the flow is sustained through a central vent area, where it is able to carry much greater volumes of magma to the surface. While these types of eruptions have been well documented in places like Kilauea in Hawaii (Richter et al., 1970), the actual evolution of basaltic conduit geometry and eruptive dynamics is poorly understood, and a conclusive theoretical treatment would greatly benefit our predictive capabilities. In this paper we review the field and theoretical evidence for the temporal evolution of a dike to a plug and the proposed theories for the conduit processes that may govern this transition. Last, we summarize the future research necessary for a comprehensive understanding of the problem.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
